                                BILL ANALYSIS                                                                                                                                                                                                   
                                                                 SB 1034


Date of Hearing:  March 8, 1994

                    ASSEMBLY COMMITTEE ON INSURANCE

                    Juanita M. McDonald, Chairwoman

          SB 1034  (Thompson) - As Amended:  January 25, 1994

SENATE ACTIONS:   

COMMITTEE SEN I. R.     VOTE 4-1   COMMITTEE          VOTE        

SENATE FLOOR:  21-12

SUBJECT

Workers' Compensation:  California State University Police Department.

DIGEST

Existing law:

1)  Provides that most local agency peace officers and sheriffs, 
š    California  Highway Patrol officers, and University of California 
    police and  firefighters disabled by an injury arising out of and in 
    the course of his  or her duties on the job are entitled to receive 
    their full salary for up  to one year in lieu of temporary disability 
    benefits under workers'  compensation.   

2)  Indicates that these payments are not subject to income tax.  

This bill:  

1)  Authorizes members of the California State University (CSU) Police  
š     Department to bargain for enhanced disability leave in lieu of 
    temporary  disability benefits under workers' compensation for up to 
    one year for an  injury arising out of and in the course of his or 
    her duty.  The enhanced  benefits would be equal to the employee's 
    net take home salary on the date  of the injury.   

2)  Specifies that the enhanced benefits are limited to officers whose  
š     principal duties consist of active law enforcement.

3)  Provides that the take home salary is the amount of salary minus 
š    federal  and state income taxes and the employee's retirement 
    contributions.   

4)  Indicates that the enhanced disability benefits would not be paid for 
š     psychological injuries or physical disabilities with a psychological 
     origin.   


