                                                                 SB 1034

FISCAL EFFECT

Unknown.

COMMENTS

1)  According to the sponsor, California School Employees' Association 
š    (CSEA),   the proposed legislation is necessary in order to provide 
    consistent  benefits to peace officers injured on the job.  The 
    sponsor indicates that  even though the CSU peace officers are 
    subject to the same occupational  dangers as peace officers already 
    covered, CSU peace officers are not  entitled to the enhanced 
    benefits.

2)  The enhanced benefits are significantly more generous than temporary  
š     disability benefits under the workers' compensation program.  
    Temporary  disability benefits are currently paid at a rate equal to 
    two-thirds of the  employee's wages.  Under the recently enacted 
    reform package, temporary  disability payments will increase from a 
    current weekly maximum of $336 to  $406 on July 1, 1994, $448 on July 
    1, 1995, and $490 on July 1, 1996.

3)  Assembly Bill 352 (Tucker, 1993) which extended enhanced benefits to  
š     probation officers, group counselors and supervisors if the 
    disability  resulted from a violent act against the employee in the 
    course of his or  duties passed out of this committee this year (7-
    0).  The provisions of
   AB 352 are only available if approved by a memorandum of understanding 
     under a collective bargaining agreement.


Sponsor:  California School Employees' Association

Support:  Peace Officers Research Association of California
         Statewide University Police Association

Opposition:  None Received.  
















